<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erebuni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">782 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Erebuni Fortress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erebuni is an Urartian fortress on the Arin Berd hill in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription records its founding by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a> in 782 BCE, and it served as an Urartian administrative and military centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a> plain. Excavations since 1950 have uncovered a palace, temples and wall paintings, and the name Erebuni is regarded as the origin of Yerevan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erebuni.jpg" alt="Erebuni" width="320" height="114" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Armen Manukov · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erebuni_Fortress,_Yerevan,_Armenia_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
